# **Part V: The Trajectory — Where This Is Going**

The question the record raises, having followed two people across 220 days, is: what happens next? The record ends on March 9. Based on the identified trends and the psychological dynamics, the following trajectories can be assessed.

**Scenario A: Nothing Changes (Most Likely in Short Term)**

The most probable immediate outcome is that the dynamic continues in its current compressed form. Annie continues to provide minimal contact. Dan continues to cycle between crisis and return. The drug supply chain continues to provide the structural reason for ongoing contact. Both parties suffer.

This scenario is most likely because nothing in the final weeks of the record suggests either party has found the leverage to break the pattern. Annie has not found the courage to be honest. Dan has not found the capacity to exit without her confession. The March 9 record — drug coordination as if the previous night's goodbye had not happened — is the clearest possible evidence that the default state is continuation.

**Scenario B: An External Event Forces Resolution**

Several real events are bearing down on this dynamic from outside. Annie's grandmother is on hospice — her death will change Annie's caregiving burden and her family's attention landscape significantly. Annie's parents were out of town for portions of the final two weeks, and their return appeared to constrain her. Any significant change to Annie's external circumstances — the end of a caregiving obligation, a family confrontation about her behavior, a change in the other relationship — could force the honesty that neither party has been able to produce voluntarily.

Dan's physical health is also a legitimate concern. He references throwing up blood on February 22. He is using drugs daily. He is not sleeping. He is in a state of sustained stress that is documented to have physiological consequences. A health crisis — his or Annie's — would force the conversation that text messages have so far failed to produce.

**Scenario C: Dan Exits, Partially or Fully**

The record contains something remarkable and easy to miss: Dan has gotten better at articulating what he needs. His final substantive messages — 'I accept your choice. I love you so much that it changed my life. That's it' — are more precise, less reactive, and more genuinely final than anything in the first months of the record. He has, despite everything, been developing a clearer picture of what the situation is and what it requires.

The February 27-28 messages may represent a genuine threshold that was interrupted before it could complete. If Dan were given — or could give himself — enough separation from Annie to let that grief process finish, he might find he has already done most of the work. The record ends with him apologizing for speaking. It also ends with him, hours earlier, writing one of the most self-possessed and genuinely loving exit statements in the record: 'Please accept yourself and stop looking for the hidden Annie Ulmer that you haven't found yet. That person is wonderful.' That is not the voice of a destroyed man. That is the voice of a man who, when not in crisis, can see clearly.

